  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 827 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nWinds will decrease through the evening as the upper-level low\nmoves into the Central-Southern Plains. Some fog may be present in\nthe southern Gila National Forest in Grant County this morning \ndue to recent rains, but lower dew points will be moving into our \narea on Tuesday, keeping conditions mainly dry. Temperatures for \nTuesday will be 6 to 8 degrees below normal for early November\n(mid 60s in the lowlands). The NBM had El Paso be about 5 degrees\ncooler than what the MOS guidance for both Tuesday and Wednesday\ndue to increasing pressure highs aloft allowing for faster diurnal\nheating from the sun, despite light cold air advection from the \nnorth.\n\nTemperatures for Wednesday will be relatively similar to Tuesday,\nif not slightly warmer. Winds will increase slightly from the \nwest to about 10-15 mph. An upper-level low moving across the\nCentral Rockies will bring a Pacific cold front through the area\nWednesday afternoon, which will cool temperatures down slightly \nfor Thursday. Both models have the low too far north for \nprecipitation to affect the region. For the rest of the week, \nconditions will be relatively dry and quiet until Friday, where\nthe Borderland could see a possible chance of rain--confidence is\nlow at this time, and the ECMWF is leaning on the wetter side of\nthings. I am skeptical if we will get any rain at the end of the \nweek.",
